      en: "The Tamga-Tash Inscriptions in Jeti-Ögüz District, Issyk-Kul Region, are rock carvings from the Karakhanid period (10th-13th centuries). They contain runic scripts, not yet fully deciphered, as well as symbols. The name means 'marked stone'. The site is in a narrow valley near the Barskoon River. The inscriptions are important evidence of the spread of Islam and Turkic literacy in the region. They are threatened by weather and have been documented since 2005.",
